## Package locks

Wireit will now treat any `package-lock.json` files found in the script's package directory, or in any of its parent directories, as though they were input files.

This way, if you run `npm install`, `npm upgrade`, etc. -- all of the scripts that could be affected by those new dependencies will automatically become stale.

Since some users might be using Yarn, pnpm, or some other npm-alternative, you can specify which filename to look for by setting `packageLocks`. You can also use this setting to disable the behavior entirely, by setting it to `[]`:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "wireit"
  },
  "wireit": {
    "build": {
      "command": "tsc",
      "files": ["src/**/*.ts", "tsconfig.json"],
      "output": ["lib/**"],
      "packageLocks": ["yarn.lock"]
    }
  }
}
```

I think for users using npm alternatives, they'll want an easier way to set `packageLocks` for all scripts in a package, so I've filed https://github.com/lit/wireit/issues/66 to provide a way to more conveniently set options for all scripts in a package.

Fixes https://github.com/lit/wireit/issues/27

## Also

- Fix a bug in watch mode, where we were only listening for `change` events, and not `add` events, which meant that creating a new file did not trigger a watch until it was modified again.

Test to catch any off by one checks in `toSpliced`. e.g:

```diff
- if (newLen >= Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER) {
+ if (newLen > Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER) {
      throw new TypeError();
  }
```
